<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matter of Britain</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Matter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> is the body of medieval legend about the early history of Britain, above all King Arthur and his knights, Merlin and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-grail">Holy Grail</a>. Built on Welsh and Breton traditions and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-of-monmouth">Geoffrey of Monmouth</a>'s History of the Kings of Britain of about 1136, it was developed by French poets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chretien-de-troyes">Chrétien de Troyes</a> and spread across European literature. The term was first used by the poet Jean Bodel.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Matter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> was one of the three great Western story cycles recalled repeatedly in medieval literature, with the Matter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, concerning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> and his companions, and the Matter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, drawn from classical mythology and history. The poet Jean Bodel named the three in his Chanson des Saisnes, writing that there are only three subject matters: that of France, that of Britain and that of great Rome. Its chronicles and chivalric romances, in prose and verse, flourished from the 12th to the 16th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-of-monmouth">Geoffrey of Monmouth</a>'s 12th-century Historia Regum Britanniae is a central component. Geoffrey drew on earlier British texts, including the 9th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/historia-brittonum">Historia Brittonum</a>, the earliest known source of the story of Brutus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>, which linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to the heroes scattered after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>. He listed Coel Hen as a king of the Britons whose daughter Helena bore the future Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a>, and he told how the post-Roman Britons under Arthur briefly conquered much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. This pseudo-history gave the new Norman England a body of national myth, portraying the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a> as a restoration of the Britain of the Celtic Britons.</p><p>The Arthurian legend is the best-known part. Geoffrey and his successor Wace, together with Welsh and Breton tales such as the Mabinogion, became the foundation for the Arthurian romances that first emerged in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the second half of the 12th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chretien-de-troyes">Chrétien de Troyes</a> and Robert de Boron introduced or popularised many of its motifs, among them the Grail, Camelot, Excalibur, Merlin and the love of Lancelot and Guinevere. The Vulgate Cycle of the early 13th century set out Arthur's kingdom and the quest for the Grail, in which Galahad, Perceval and Bors succeed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Thomas Malory's 15th-century Le Morte d'Arthur established an English-language quasi-canon based on the French prose cycles. Interest largely waned by the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and survived mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and, to some degree, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> into the 17th century. The 19th-century Romantic revival brought the legend back, chiefly in Malory's telling.</p><p>Since the Celtic Revival, scholars have tried to link the Arthurian tales to Celtic mythology, identifying Morgan le Fay with the Welsh goddess Modron or the Irish Morrígan. Other schools read the Grail tradition as an allegory of spiritual growth, as Joseph Campbell did, or traced it to ritual, as Jessie L. Weston did in From Ritual to Romance in 1920, an interpretation no longer fashionable. Whether Arthur existed as a historical figure remains a long-running debate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Matter of Britain” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Breton storytellers helped carry the <b>Matter of Britain</b> into French literature: Marie de France presented her twelfth-century lais as Breton tales, and the forest of Brocéliande became a setting of Arthurian romance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
